  Confidentiality:  Only authorised people can see the data.
Integrity: The data is accurate and complete, and it hasn't been changed. A CT scan missing its write-up is a great example: the data still exists, 
  but it can't be trusted.
Availability: The data has to be there when it's needed. The penicillin example shows why that matters.
  What it protects	Example controls
C	Who can see it	Encryption, passwords and MFA, access permissions
I	Whether it's been changed	Hashing, digital signatures, checksums
A	Whether it's there when needed	Backups, redundant servers, patching, fault tolerance.

 AAA  Here's an analogy for all three. You're at a nightclub:

Authentication: the bouncer checks your ID.
Authorisation: your wristband decides whether you can get into the VIP area.
Accounting: the cameras record when you came in, where you went, and when you left.

